<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International English</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Communication Skills Are Important in the Workplace?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Take basic notes on a text about a familiar topic. Reading: Recognize the writer's point of view in various texts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can take introductory notes on articles about English as a global language, recording main ideas (e.g., "English connects people from different cultures in business")<br>Can recognize a writer's viewpoint on the spread of English, noting arguments about its importance in international business (e.g., "The article highlights that sectors like logistics and finance in Panama rely heavily on English for communication.")<br>Can summarize points from texts on English in the workplace, preparing for discussions on language learning (e.g., "The text argues that English skills can enhance career opportunities.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various international workplaces (e.g., a UN conference, a multinational company's office in Panama, a logistics hub). Students work in pairs to quickly list 3-5 communication skills they think are essential in these settings. The teacher asks, "Why has English become such an important language for international business in these scenarios?" prompting students to recall vocabulary from Lesson 1 like 'global,' 'interactions,' 'cross-cultural communication.' (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes "Effective communication skills help us succeed globally" on the board. The teacher models the pronunciation of 'effective' and 'globally,' focusing on the long /e/ sound in 'effective' and the reduced vowel in 'globally' /'ɡloʊ.bəl.i/. The teacher briefly explains how recognizing a writer's viewpoint helps understand the full message of a text. For example, "When someone says 'English *must* be learned,' they are strongly emphasizing its necessity, which tells us their point of view." (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a text says 'English is *vital* for business,' is the writer saying it's just 'nice to have' or 'absolutely necessary'?" (Expected: Absolutely necessary)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I ask you to take notes, what should you write down: every single word, or just the main ideas and important details?" (Expected: Main ideas and important details)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: e.g., 8:00 AM - 8:15 AM]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International English".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short article (approx. 250-300 words) titled "The Lingua Franca of Global Business: English in the Modern Workplace." The article is a semi-authentic informational text (register: formal, genre: newspaper editorial/online article), containing target vocabulary (e.g., 'lingua franca,' 'global,' 'cross-cultural communication,' 'enhance,' 'proficient') and grammar (e.g., "English has become an international language," "employees *must* communicate effectively," "Can you explain which skills *can* help you advance your career?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student will be able to comprehend and analyze written texts about English's role in the workplace to extract key information and understand perspectives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> Students quickly read the article and answer: "What is the main idea the writer wants us to understand about English in the workplace?" (1 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read the article again to determine if the following statements are True or False (teacher provides 3-4 statements, e.g., "The article claims English is only useful in diplomacy, not business."). They must correct the false statements based on the text. (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match paragraphs (A, B, C) with their main topic from a given list (e.g., "The historical spread of English," "Benefits of English fluency for employees," "Challenges in cross-cultural settings"). (4 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: e.g., 8:15 AM - 8:25 AM]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Reading.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying main ideas, distinguishing factual information from the writer's opinion, and noting specific details using Present Perfect, modals for necessity, and indirect questions. Vocabulary: 'communication,' 'skills,' 'fluency,' 'business,' 'cross-cultural communication,' 'enhance,' 'recognize,' 'relevant,' 'necessary.' Pronunciation focus on /e/ in 'relevant,' 'necessary' and reduced vowels in 'communication,' 'accessible.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Note-taking for Main Ideas:</b> The teacher instructs students to re-read the article, highlighting or underlining key phrases and sentences that represent the main idea of each paragraph. Then, students write one concise sentence for each paragraph, summarizing its main point in their own words. Example: "Paragraph 1: English has become the global lingua franca for business communication, connecting diverse backgrounds." (4 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Recognizing Writer's Point of View:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 statements related to the text. Students identify if each statement reflects a factual piece of information from the article or the writer's opinion/point of view. Students must justify their answer by pointing to specific words or phrases (e.g., use of modals like 'must' or adjectives like 'vital,' 'important'). For instance, "English *could* help you access more academic resources" (factual possibility based on access) vs. "English *must* be a core skill for all future employees" (writer's strong opinion on necessity). (4 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Summarizing with Indirect Questions:</b> In pairs, students use their notes to ask and answer indirect questions about the text and the writer's viewpoint. For example, Student A: "Can you explain what the article says about the importance of cross-cultural communication?" Student B: "The article highlights that it's necessary to adapt language for different audiences when English is used globally." (6 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: e.g., 8:25 AM - 8:39 AM]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students engage in a communicative task where they use the language in a realistic scenario of "International English" and the theme "What Communication Skills Are Important in the Workplace?".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Summary Presentation. Students work in groups of three. They are given an additional short paragraph (or a modified version of the original article focusing on a specific workplace skill). Each group must: 1) Read the new text carefully. 2) Individually take notes on the main ideas and any arguments presented by the writer. 3) Collaboratively synthesize their notes to create a bulleted list of 3-4 key takeaways about the communication skills discussed and the writer's overall message. 4) Prepare to briefly present their summary to another group, using target vocabulary (e.g., 'proficient,' 'relevant,' 'enhance') and grammar (e.g., "The writer has emphasized that...", "Employees *should* consider...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in discussing the article's content, collaborative interaction during note synthesis, and communicative task completion (creating a clear summary and presenting it). Emphasis is on effectively conveying the main points and the writer's perspective from the text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: e.g., 8:39 AM - 8:50 AM]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and summary presentation, focusing on reading comprehension and note-taking skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students produce their group's bulleted summary list. The teacher observes their interaction and final presentation to another group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy and completeness of notes (Do they capture main ideas?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify the writer's point of view in the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'fluency,' 'global,' 'enhance') and grammar (Present Perfect, modals for necessity).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and conciseness of the summarized points.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective collaboration within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new idea you learned today about English in the workplace from the texts?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you now in taking notes on a text to identify its main ideas and the writer's viewpoint? (1=not confident, 5=very confident)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: e.g., 8:50 AM - 8:55 AM]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did identifying the writer's point of view help you understand the articles better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What communication skill, mentioned in the texts, do you think is most relevant for *your* future career, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of engaging students with the reading tasks? Were students able to effectively take notes and recognize the writer's viewpoint? What adjustments are needed, especially regarding vocabulary support or explaining complex sentences for B1.2 learners? Were the pronunciation points successfully integrated? Some students struggled with indirect questions; I could provide more structured sentence starters next time. The group work fostered good discussions, but some groups focused less on the writer's specific opinion and more on general facts. I need to scaffold the distinction more clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction):</b> In the next lesson, students will use the notes and summaries they created today as a basis for a debate or structured discussion about "Why English proficiency is essential for career advancement in Panama," directly applying their understanding of the texts and engaging in active speaking using the target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: e.g., 8:55 AM - 9:00 AM]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>